<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huliaipole</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulyaipole, Hulyaipole</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huliaipole is a town in Zaporizhzhia Oblast in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. It was the birthplace and headquarters of the anarchist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno">Nestor Makhno</a>, whose Revolutionary Insurrectionary Army of Ukraine was based there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> of 1917–1921. The town became the centre of the anarchist zone sometimes called the Free Territory, and changed hands repeatedly between Makhnovists, Whites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Huliaipole, whose Ukrainian name means walk-about field, lies in the valley of the Haichul river in Polohy Raion of Zaporizhzhia Oblast. The name of the river is derived from Turkic words meaning free and steppe. The town is 98 kilometres from the regional centre of Zaporizhzhia and 8 kilometres from the railway station of the same name. It is a small city; in January 2022 its estimated population was 12,786.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed the Crimean Khanate, the area was settled mostly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a> and the nomadic Lesser Nogai Horde. The settlement arose in the 1770s after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> defensive line was built on former lands of the Zaporozhian Sich. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> dissolved the Sich, local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> fled or were brought into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> under the Shabelskiys. In 1785 the Yekaterinoslav Governorate ordered a state military settlement founded here for protection from the Crimean Khanate. Its name reflected the fairs that had long been held on the spot.</p><p>From the middle of the nineteenth century the village became a commercial and industrial town. The first trades fair took place in 1859, with more than fifty trading companies and a turnover of 1 million rubles. Factories making agricultural machinery opened from 1882, and the Chaplyne–Berdyansk railway was laid nearby in 1898. Between 1917 and 1921, in the turbulence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> and the Ukrainian War of Independence, the town changed hands no fewer than sixteen times, among Austro-Hungarian forces, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, the Ukrainian People's Republic, the Hetmanate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-denikin">Anton Denikin</a>'s White Army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno">Nestor Makhno</a>'s Insurgent Army. It became widely known as the headquarters of the Revolutionary Insurgent Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and the capital of the Makhnovshchina.</p><p>Huliaipole gained city status in 1938 during rapid industrial development in the 1930s; at least 108 of its residents died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a> of 1932–1933. German forces occupied it on 5 October 1941, and Soviet troops retook it on 16 September 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Notable natives include Makhno himself, born in 1888, and the anarchists Semen Karetnyk, Oleksiy Marchenko and Voldemar Antoni, who founded the Union of Poor Peasants. After Ukrainian independence the town's economy rested on an agricultural machinery plant, a paint plant, a shoe factory and food enterprises. In 2020 the city became part of Polohy Raion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huliaipole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nestor Makhno, an anarchist from <b>Huliaipole</b>, organised peasant partisans against the Austro-German occupiers and the Ukrainian State in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-territory">Free Territory</a></li></ul></section>
